#v(-4mm)
In recent years, there has been a lot of interest in the idea of _optimism_ in
learning algorithms. We build on the #lecture-link("learning1", <ftrl-omd-general-case>)[FTRL and OMD updates and regret bounds]. The fundamental idea behind optimism is the following:

#v(-1mm)
#h(1mm)#box(
  width: 99%,
  stroke: (left: .4mm + gray),
  inset: (left: 3mm, top: 0mm, bottom: 1mm),
)[_When all players learn at the same time, the environment is nonstationary but not necessarily adversarial. Can one then take advantage of this to design learning algorithms with better regret guarantees and convergence properties?_]

= Predictivity, optimism, and acceleration <sec-predictivity>

The idea of predictivity is to _anticipate_ the next utility gradient $vg^((t+1))$ by
having a _prediction_ $vm^((t+1))$.

At least three variants can be defined.

#block(breakable: false, table(
  columns: (22%, 78%),
  align: (horizon + left, horizon + left),
  inset: (y: 1.4mm, x: 2mm),
  fill: (j, i) => if j == 1 and i in (2, 4, 5) {
    blue.lighten(95%)
  } else {
    none
  },
  stroke: (j, i) => (
    top: if i == 0 { black + .4mm } else { none },
    bottom: if i == 0 or i == 5 { black + .4mm } else { gray + .2mm },
    left: if j == 1 { gray + .2mm } else { none },
  ),
  table.header()[*Algorithm / variant*][*Update*],
  [*FTRL* \ Non-predictive],
  $
    vx^((t+1)) := argmax_(vx in X) {ip(sum_(tau = 1)^t vg^((tau)), vx) - 1 / eta psi(vx)}
  $,

  [*FTRL* \ Predictive],
  $
    vx^((t+1)) := argmax_(vx in X) { ip(#html-math-color(darkblue, $vm^((t+1))$) + sum_(tau = 1)^t vg^((tau)), vx) - 1 / eta psi(vx) }
  $,

  [*OMD* \ Non-predictive],
  $
    vx^((t+1)) := argmax_(vx in X) {ip(vg^((t)), vx) - 1 / eta upright("D")_psi (vx || vx^((t)))}
  $,

  [*OMD* \ Predictive / non-reflected],
  $
    vz^((t+1)) &:= argmax_(vz in X) {ip(vg^((t)), vz) - 1 / eta upright("D")_psi (vz || vz^((t)))} \
    vx^((t+1)) &:= argmax_(vx in X) { ip(#html-math-color(darkblue, $vm^((t+1))$), vx) - 1 / eta upright("D")_psi (vx || vz^((t+1))) }
  $,

  [*OMD* \ Predictive / reflected],
  $
    vx^((t+1)) := argmax_(vx in X) { ip(vg^((t)) + #html-math-color(darkblue)[$vm^((t+1)) - vm^((t))$], vx) - 1 / eta upright("D")_psi (vx || vx^((t))) }
  $,
))

While the three predictive algorithms are in general different, they coincide in the special case of _Legendre regularizers_ (see also the #lecture-link("learning1", <ftrl-omd-general-case>)[discussion of when FTRL and OMD agree]).

#remark[
  For Legendre regularizers (i.e., when $psi$'s gradients go to infinity at the boundary of $cX$), the three predictive algorithms (FTRL, non-reflected OMD, reflected OMD) coincide.
]
It is also worth noting that the predictive versions of the algorithms subsume the non-predictive versions as a special case, as we point out in the next remark.

#remark[
  The standard (non-predictive) FTRL and OMD algorithms correspond to the case where the prediction is set
  to zero, i.e., $vm^((t)) = 0$ at all times $t$.
]

== Optimism <sec-optimism>

The idea of optimism is to use predictivity with the specific guess $vm^((t+1)) = vg^((t))$ at
all times $t$. This corresponds to predicting that the feedback is slow-changing. We discuss in @sec-slow-feedback why this is a natural prediction when the feedback is generated by other learning agents. Plugging the guess into the table, predictive FTRL counts the latest feedback twice, and reflected OMD steps along the latest feedback plus its most recent change:
$
  vm^((t+1)) + sum_(tau=1)^t vg^((tau)) = sum_(tau=1)^t vg^((tau)) + vg^((t)), qquad qquad vg^((t)) + vm^((t+1)) - vm^((t)) = 2 vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1)).
$

#example[Optimistic online gradient ascent][
  The non-reflected OMD algorithm instantiated with squared Euclidean norm $psi(vx) = 1/2 norm(vx)_2^2$ gives rise to the (non-reflected) _optimistic online gradient ascent_ algorithm, whose update rule is
  #set math.equation(numbering: "(1)")
  $
    vz^((t+1)) & := #text(size: 14pt, $Pi$) _(cX)(vz^((t)) + eta vg^((t))), qquad qquad
                 vx^((t+1)) & := #text(size: 14pt, $Pi$) _(cX)(vz^((t+1)) + eta vg^((t))).
  $ <eq-ogda>
  #set math.equation(numbering: none)
]

#example[Optimistic MWU][
  For the MWU algorithm, the optimistic version of FTRL, non-reflected OMD, and reflected OMD all coincide, and give rise to the following update rule:
  $
    vx^((t+1)) prop exp(eta vr^((t)) + eta (#html-math-color(black, $vr^((t)) - vr^((t-1))$))).
  $
]

In two-player zero-sum games, optimism can stabilize learning dynamics and promote convergence under suitable conditions. In the following example, it acts as a form of _negative_ momentum that pushes the iterates towards the equilibrium; we explain the mechanism in @sec-negative-momentum, right after the example. We will give a quantitative analysis of the effect of optimism in the convergence of learning algorithms in @sec-iterate-convergence.

#example[
  The following plots show the dynamics of the optimistic and non-optimistic versions of MWU and OGD in the #lecture-link("learning1", <sec-ogd>)[two-player zero-sum example used to compare OGD and MWU], whose utility matrix is
  $ U_1 := mat(2, 1; 0, 2). $
  The multiplicative weights update algorithm was set up with constant learning rate $eta = 0.25$, while the online gradient descent algorithm was set up with learning rate $eta = 0.1$.
  #align(center, image(
    "figures/learning2/optimistic.svg",
    width: 72%,
    alt: "Comparison of optimistic and non-optimistic MWU and OGD trajectories.",
  ))
  The axes show the probabilities $x_2^((t))$ and $y_2^((t))$ that the players assign to their second actions. The purple dot is the starting point, the black dot is the unique Nash equilibrium $vx^* = (2\/3, 1\/3)$, $vy^* = (1\/3, 2\/3)$, and the gray dotted line tracks the _average_ strategies. The optimistic version of OGD is the non-reflected update (@eq-ogda). The optimistic dynamics converge to the equilibrium, while the non-optimistic ones spiral away from it.
]

== Why does optimism help? Negative momentum <sec-negative-momentum>

The figure above shows optimism pushing the iterates towards the equilibrium. This may look paradoxical: if optimism predicts that the feedback will not change, where does a systematic push towards the equilibrium come from? The answer is that optimism does not use its prediction to stand still. It uses it to take the next step _early_, and then corrects with the _change_ in the feedback, $vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1))$. In zero-sum games, this change carries information about the direction of the equilibrium that plain gradient dynamics discard. We make this precise for _unconstrained bilinear two-player zero-sum games_ with the Euclidean regularizer. The argument is specific to this setting; it is an explanation of the figure above, not a statement about predictive learning in arbitrary environments.

#block(breakable: false)[
#paragraph-marker() *Optimism as negative momentum.*~~
When $cX = RR^d$ and $psi = 1/2 norm(dot.c)_2^2$, all predictive variants coincide and there are no projections. Eliminating $vz$ from (@eq-ogda), optimistic gradient ascent becomes
#set math.equation(numbering: "(1)")
$
  vx^((t+1)) = vx^((t)) + 2 eta vg^((t)) - eta vg^((t-1)) = vx^((t)) + eta vg^((t)) + eta (vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1))).
$ <eq-oga-unconstrained>
]
#set math.equation(numbering: none)
For plain gradient ascent, the last displacement is $vx^((t)) - vx^((t-1)) = eta vg^((t-1))$. So optimism takes the current gradient step twice and _undoes the previous gradient step_: it attaches a momentum coefficient of $-1$ to the last gradient step. This is the sense in which optimism is a form of _negative momentum_. (Heavy-ball momentum with a negative coefficient on the last _displacement_ is a related but different method; Gidel et al.~#citep(<gidel2019negative>) study it in games and show that it stabilizes bilinear games when combined with alternating updates.)

#paragraph-marker() *Why plain gradient dynamics spiral outwards.*~~
Consider an unconstrained bilinear zero-sum game, $u_1(vx, vy) = vx^top mU vy = -u_2(vx, vy)$. Stack the strategies as $vz = (vx, vy)$ and the feedback as
$
  F(vz) := (vg_1, vg_2) = (mU vy, -mU^top vx) = mA vz, qquad mA := mat(bold(0), mU; -mU^top, bold(0)).
$
The Nash equilibria are the points $vz^*$ with $mA vz^* = bold(0)$, and since $F(vz) = mA (vz - vz^*)$ we may translate and assume $vz^* = bold(0)$. The key structural fact is that $mA$ is skew-symmetric, so $ip(vw, mA vw) = 0$ for every $vw$: the feedback is always _orthogonal_ to the displacement from the equilibrium. Each player is pushed sideways, chasing the other, rather than towards the equilibrium. For simultaneous gradient ascent $vz^((t+1)) = vz^((t)) + eta mA vz^((t))$, this gives
$
  norm(vz^((t+1)))_2^2 = norm(vz^((t)))_2^2 + 2 eta underbrace(ip(vz^((t)), mA vz^((t))), = 0) + eta^2 norm(mA vz^((t)))_2^2 = norm(vz^((t)))_2^2 + eta^2 norm(mA vz^((t)))_2^2,
$
so the distance from the equilibrium _increases_ at every step unless the players are already at equilibrium: a step along the tangent of a circle always lands outside it. This is what the non-optimistic panels in the figure above show.

#paragraph-marker() *What the right correction looks like.*~~
The problem is that the gradient is evaluated at the current point, while the field rotates as the iterate moves. Suppose a player could instead use the feedback at the point it is about to move to, $vz^((t)) + eta F(vz^((t)))$. This is the extragradient method of Korpelevich~#citep(<korpelevich1976extragradient>):
$
  vz^((t+1)) = vz^((t)) + eta F(vz^((t)) + eta F(vz^((t)))) = vz^((t)) + eta mA vz^((t)) + eta^2 mA^2 vz^((t)).
$
The extra term points towards the equilibrium: $mA^2 = -mA^top mA$ is negative semidefinite, and $ip(vz, mA^2 vz) = -norm(mA vz)_2^2$. Expanding the square and using $ip(vz, mA vz) = ip(mA vz, mA^2 vz) = 0$,
$
  norm(vz^((t+1)))_2^2 = norm(vz^((t)))_2^2 - eta^2 norm(mA vz^((t)))_2^2 + eta^4 norm(mA^2 vz^((t)))_2^2 <= norm(vz^((t)))_2^2 - eta^2 (1 - eta^2 norm(mU)_2^2) norm(mA vz^((t)))_2^2,
$
where we used $norm(mA)_2 = norm(mU)_2$. The inward pull is of order $eta^2$, the same order as the outward drift of gradient ascent but with the opposite sign, and it wins whenever $eta norm(mU)_2 < 1$.

#paragraph-marker() *Optimism is a look-ahead computed from the past.*~~
Extragradient needs a second feedback query per round, which is not available in online learning. Optimism obtains the same correction from the last two observed feedbacks. By (@eq-oga-unconstrained), the correction term is
$
  eta (vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1))) = eta mA (vz^((t)) - vz^((t-1))),
$
and since the last displacement satisfies $vz^((t)) - vz^((t-1)) = eta mA vz^((t)) + O(eta^2 norm(vz^((t)))_2)$, the correction equals $eta^2 mA^2 vz^((t)) + O(eta^3 norm(vz^((t)))_2)$: the inward term of extragradient, to leading order. This is where the prediction enters. The vector $2 vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1)) = vg^((t)) + (vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1)))$ is the _linear extrapolation_ of the feedback sequence one step into the future, an estimate of the feedback at the point the player is heading to. When the feedback changes slowly and smoothly, this extrapolation is accurate, and it encodes exactly the rotation of the field that pushes the iterates inwards. This "extragradient from the past" is the method of Popov~#citep(<popov1980modification>). Mokhtari et al.~#citep(<mokhtari2020unified>) analyze both extragradient and optimistic gradient methods as approximations of the _proximal point method_ $vz^((t+1)) = vz^((t)) + eta F(vz^((t+1)))$, which uses the feedback at the very point it lands on.

In summary: plain gradients move tangentially around the equilibrium, and the optimistic correction supplies an inward component, because $mA^2$ is negative semidefinite.

#paragraph-marker() *An exact computation (optional).*~~
The heuristic above can be confirmed exactly in the simplest game. This lemma, and the remark that follows, can be skipped on a first reading.

#lemma[Optimistic vs. plain gradient ascent in a bilinear game][
  Let $sigma > 0$, and consider the game on $RR times RR$ with $u_1(x, y) = sigma x y = -u_2(x, y)$, whose unique Nash equilibrium is $(0, 0)$. Let $w^((t)) := x^((t)) + i y^((t)) in CC$.
  1. Under simultaneous gradient ascent, $|w^((t+1))| = sqrt(1 + eta^2 sigma^2) thin |w^((t))|$ for all $t$.
  2. Under optimistic gradient ascent (@eq-oga-unconstrained) with $0 < eta sigma < 1\/2$, $w^((t)) = c_+ lambda_+^t + c_- lambda_-^t$ for constants $c_plus.minus in CC$ determined by the initialization, where
  $
    lambda_plus.minus = (1 plus.minus sqrt(1 - 4 eta^2 sigma^2)) / 2 - i eta sigma, qquad |lambda_plus.minus|^2 = (1 plus.minus sqrt(1 - 4 eta^2 sigma^2)) / 2 < 1.
  $
  In particular, $w^((t)) -> 0$ linearly, with asymptotic convergence factor $|lambda_+| = 1 - eta^2 sigma^2 \/ 2 + O(eta^4 sigma^4)$.
]
#proof[
  The feedback is $g_1 = sigma y$ and $g_2 = -sigma x$; in complex notation, $g_1 + i g_2 = -i sigma w$. Gradient ascent therefore reads $w^((t+1)) = (1 - i eta sigma) w^((t))$, and $|1 - i eta sigma| = sqrt(1 + eta^2 sigma^2)$. Optimistic gradient ascent reads $w^((t+1)) = (1 - 2 i eta sigma) w^((t)) + i eta sigma w^((t-1))$, a linear recurrence with characteristic polynomial $lambda^2 - (1 - 2 i eta sigma) lambda - i eta sigma$. Its discriminant is $(1 - 2 i eta sigma)^2 + 4 i eta sigma = 1 - 4 eta^2 sigma^2 > 0$, which gives the two distinct roots $lambda_plus.minus$ above. Writing $s := sqrt(1 - 4 eta^2 sigma^2) in (0, 1)$, so that $eta^2 sigma^2 = (1 - s^2)\/4$,
  $
    |lambda_plus.minus|^2 = (1 plus.minus s)^2 / 4 + (1 - s)(1 + s) / 4 = (1 plus.minus s) / 2 < 1.
  $
  Finally, $s = 1 - 2 eta^2 sigma^2 + O(eta^4 sigma^4)$ gives the stated factor.
]

Gradient ascent moves away from the equilibrium by the factor $sqrt(1 + eta^2 sigma^2) = 1 + eta^2 sigma^2\/2 + O(eta^4 sigma^4)$ at every step, while optimism converges with asymptotic factor $1 - eta^2 sigma^2\/2 + O(eta^4 sigma^4)$: the same order, with the sign flipped.

#remark[
  Every unconstrained bilinear game reduces to the lemma. If $mU = mP mSigma mQ^top$ is a singular value decomposition, the orthogonal change of variables $vx' = mP^top vx$, $vy' = mQ^top vy$ commutes with gradient steps and decouples the game into independent scalar games $sigma_k x'_k y'_k$; coordinates with no nonzero singular value receive zero feedback and stay put. Hence, for $eta norm(mU)_2 < 1\/2$, optimistic gradient ascent converges linearly to a Nash equilibrium, at a rate governed by the smallest _nonzero_ singular value of $mU$.

  The game of the figure above is covered too. Its equilibrium is interior, so while the iterates remain in the interior, the projections onto the simplices act as projections onto their affine hulls. In the coordinates $vx = vx^* + s(1, -1)$, $vy = vy^* + r(1, -1)$, the dynamics are then exactly the scalar game of the lemma with $sigma = 3\/2$. For $eta = 0.1$, the ordinary gradient update expands the distance to the equilibrium by the factor $sqrt(1.0225) approx 1.011$ per iteration while the projections remain inactive. The optimistic dynamics have an asymptotic convergence factor of approximately $0.988$, illustrating the damping effect.
]

Beyond unconstrained bilinear games, this linear-algebraic argument no longer applies verbatim, but its accounting survives. In the computation above, the inward correction reduces the squared distance to the equilibrium by about $eta^2 norm(mA vz^((t)))_2^2$, which is the squared length of the step. The regret analysis has the same structure: the RVU bound of @sec-rvu contains the negative term $-1\/(8 eta) sum_t norm(vx^((t)) - vx^((t-1)))^2$, a credit proportional to the squared length of the player's steps, and in @sec-fast-zero-sum this credit absorbs the variation of the opponent's feedback.

== Why might feedback be slow-changing? <sec-slow-feedback>

Optimism replaces the unknown next feedback with the last one observed, so its prediction error is
$
  vg^((t+1)) - vm^((t+1)) = vg^((t+1)) - vg^((t)).
$
Optimism is therefore accurate exactly when the feedback has small _variation between consecutive rounds_. This differs from stationarity: over a long horizon, the feedback can change substantially (within its bounded range) while changing little from one round to the next. Regret bounds that scale with the variation of the feedback, rather than with the number of rounds $T$, were first obtained by Hazan and Kale~#citep(<hazan2010extracting>) (variation around the mean) and Chiang et al.~#citep(<chiang2012gradual>) (variation between consecutive rounds, the quantity relevant here). The general framework of online learning with an arbitrary prediction $vm^((t))$ was developed by Rakhlin and Sridharan in two companion papers: one on online learning #citep(<rakhlin2013online>), and one on games and saddle-point problems #citep(<rakhlin2013predictable>).

#paragraph-marker() *Intuition.*~~
Think of two players who update their mixed strategies with a small learning rate, or of two firms that adjust their prices gradually. If one player's strategy moves only slightly from one round to the next, then the other player's expected payoff from each of its actions also changes only slightly. Last round's payoffs are then a good forecast of this round's.

#paragraph-marker() *Feedback variation is controlled by the opponents' movement.*~~
In the #lecture-link("learning_intro", <def-canonical-learning>)[canonical learning setup], the feedback of Player~$i$ is a fixed function of the strategies of the other players. In a finite normal-form game,
$
  g_i^((t))(a_i) = u_i (a_i, vx_(-i)^((t))) = EE_(a_(-i) ~ times.o_(j != i) vx_j^((t))) [u_i (a_i, a_(-i))].
$
If the payoffs lie in $[-1, 1]$, then $|g_i^((t+1))(a_i) - g_i^((t))(a_i)|$ is at most the $ell_1$ distance between the product distributions $times.o_(j != i) vx_j^((t+1))$ and $times.o_(j != i) vx_j^((t))$, which in turn is at most the sum of the $ell_1$ distances of the factors (change one factor at a time and apply the triangle inequality). Hence
#set math.equation(numbering: "(1)")
$
  norm(vg_i^((t+1)) - vg_i^((t)))_oo <= sum_(j != i) norm(vx_j^((t+1)) - vx_j^((t)))_1.
$ <eq-feedback-variation>
#set math.equation(numbering: none)
In a two-player zero-sum game with payoff matrix $mU$ for Player~1, the feedback is linear, $vg_1^((t)) = mU vy^((t))$ and $vg_2^((t)) = -mU^top vx^((t))$, so that $norm(vg_1^((t+1)) - vg_1^((t)))_* <= norm(mU)_"op" norm(vy^((t+1)) - vy^((t)))$, with the operator norm defined in @sec-fast-zero-sum.

#paragraph-marker() *Stable learners move slowly.*~~
If $psi$ is 1-strongly convex with respect to $norm(dot.c)$ on $cX$, then the map $vtheta |-> argmax_(vx in cX) {ip(vtheta, vx) - 1/eta psi(vx)}$ is $eta$-Lipschitz from $norm(dot.c)_*$ to $norm(dot.c)$. Predictive FTRL evaluates this map at $vtheta^((t+1)) = vm^((t+1)) + sum_(tau=1)^t vg^((tau))$, and with the optimistic prediction $vtheta^((t+1)) - vtheta^((t)) = 2vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1))$. So, if $norm(vg^((t)))_* <= B$ at all times,
$
  norm(vx^((t+1)) - vx^((t))) <= eta norm(2 vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1)))_* <= 3 eta B.
$
For example, the negative entropy is 1-strongly convex with respect to $norm(dot.c)_1$ on the simplex (Pinsker's inequality). So if all $n$ players of a game with payoffs in $[-1,1]$ use optimistic MWU with learning rate $eta$, then $norm(vx_j^((t+1)) - vx_j^((t)))_1 <= 3 eta$ for every $j$, and by (@eq-feedback-variation),
$
  norm(vg_i^((t+1)) - vg_i^((t)))_oo <= 3 (n - 1) eta quad "for every player" i "and time" t.
$

#paragraph-marker() *Consequence for regret.*~~
Under these assumptions (bounded feedback, strongly convex regularizers, and every player using such a stable update), slow variation is a consequence of the dynamics rather than an assumption about the environment. It is not automatic in games, as discussed below. The payoff is quantitative. The prediction error of optimism is $O(eta)$, whereas the non-predictive choice $vm^((t)) = 0$ has prediction error $vg^((t))$, of order $1$. In the RVU bound of @sec-rvu, the term $eta sum_t norm(vg^((t)) - vm^((t)))_*^2$ therefore shrinks from $O(eta T)$ to $O(eta^3 T)$, and with $eta = Theta(T^(-1\/4))$ each player's regret is $O(T^(1\/4))$ instead of $O(T^(1\/2))$. This is the mechanism behind the fast rates of Syrgkanis et al.~#citep(<syrgkanis2015fast>).

#paragraph-marker() *Beyond normal-form games, and in practice.*~~
The same reasoning applies whenever the feedback is an $L$-Lipschitz function $F$ of the iterates, as in smooth convex-concave saddle-point problems and smooth games: $norm(F(vz^((t+1))) - F(vz^((t))))_* <= L norm(vz^((t+1)) - vz^((t)))$. In that setting, optimistic mirror descent is closely related to the extragradient method and its mirror-prox generalization #citep(<rakhlin2013predictable>), as seen in @sec-negative-momentum. Slowly varying feedback is also exploited in practice. Predictive regret matching and predictive CFR#super[+], which use the last observed feedback as the prediction, converge faster than the best previous CFR variants on most of the eighteen benchmark games examined by Farina et al.~#citep(<farina2021faster>), and optimistic gradient methods were proposed to stabilize the training of generative adversarial networks #citep(<daskalakis2018training>).

#paragraph-marker() *When the last feedback is a poor prediction.*~~
Slow variation is not guaranteed.
- _Sampled feedback._ The argument above concerns the expected utility vector. If a player only observes a utility computed from sampled opponent actions, or bandit feedback, then $vg^((t+1)) - vg^((t))$ contains sampling noise that does not vanish as $eta -> 0$. For noisy but stationary feedback, the running average of past feedback is a better prediction than the last one; this is the setting of the variance bounds of Hazan and Kale~#citep(<hazan2010extracting>) and of the first Rakhlin--Sridharan paper #citep(<rakhlin2013online>).
- _Unstable opponents._ An opponent that plays exact best responses, as in best-response dynamics, can switch between pure strategies from one round to the next, and the bound (@eq-feedback-variation) gives nothing.
- _Large learning rates or changing games._ The stability estimate scales with $eta$, and the bound (@eq-feedback-variation) assumes a fixed game.
In these cases, the guarantee degrades but stays controlled. The RVU bound depends on the _realized_ prediction errors, and since $norm(vg^((t)) - vg^((t-1)))_* <= 2B$, the prediction-error term is never larger than $4 eta B^2 T$, of the same order as the worst-case bound $eta B^2 T$ of the non-predictive choice. This is a comparison of worst-case bounds, not of individual sequences: if, say, $vg^((t)) = bold(0)$ while $vg^((t-1))$ is large, the non-predictive error is zero while the optimistic error is not. In short, optimistic methods keep a bounded worst-case prediction-error term, and benefit substantially when the realized variation is small.

== Predictive regret bounds (RVU) <sec-rvu>

Intuitively, one would expect that predictions _help_ in reducing the regret of the learning algorithm.
At one extreme, one would presumably hope that if the prediction is perfect, then the regret would be very small.
This is indeed the case, as shown by Syrgkanis et al.~#citep(<syrgkanis2015fast>).

#theorem[RVU bound, #citep(<syrgkanis2015fast>)][
  Let $cX$ be convex and compact, let $psi$ be 1-strongly convex on $cX$ with respect to a norm $norm(dot.c)$, with dual norm $norm(dot.c)_*$, and let $Omega := max_(cX) psi - min_(cX) psi$. Consider predictive FTRL, or non-reflected predictive OMD initialized at $vz^((1)) := argmin_(vz in cX) psi(vz)$, with learning rate $eta > 0$ and arbitrary predictions $vm^((t))$. Then, for every sequence of utility gradients $vg^((1)), dots, vg^((T))$, the regret satisfies the following bound, often called the _RVU bound_ (regret bounded by variation in utilities):
  $
    "Reg"^((T)) <= Omega / eta + eta sum_(t=1)^T norm(vg^((t)) - vm^((t)))_*^2 - 1 / (8 eta) sum_(t=2)^T norm(vx^((t)) - vx^((t-1)))^2.
  $
  For predictive FTRL, the constant $1\/(8 eta)$ in the last term can be improved to $1\/(4 eta)$. For Legendre regularizers, reflected OMD coincides with the other two variants, so the bound applies to it as well.
]

#remark[
  A consequence of the previous regret bound is the fact that---assuming $vm^((t)) = vg^((t))$ is _omniscient_---the regret of the learning algorithm _does not grow with time_.]

== Accelerated learning of Nash equilibria in two-player zero-sum games <sec-fast-zero-sum>

As noted by Syrgkanis et al.~#citep(<syrgkanis2015fast>), the RVU bound implies accelerated convergence to Nash equilibria in two-player zero-sum games. The proof is quite elementary, and we present it next.

Let Player~1 measure its strategies with a norm $norm(dot.c)_cX$ on $RR^m$ and Player~2 with a norm $norm(dot.c)_cY$ on $RR^n$, with dual norms $norm(dot.c)_(cX,*)$ and $norm(dot.c)_(cY,*)$. For the utility matrix $mU in RR^(m times n)$ of Player~1, define the operator norm
$
  norm(mU)_"op" := max_(vv != bold(0)) norm(mU vv)_(cX,*) / norm(vv)_cY = max_(vw != bold(0)) norm(mU^top vw)_(cY,*) / norm(vw)_cX,
$
where the two expressions agree because both equal $max ip(vw, mU vv)$ over $norm(vw)_cX <= 1$ and $norm(vv)_cY <= 1$.

#theorem[Accelerated convergence to Nash equilibria in two-player zero-sum games, #citep(<syrgkanis2015fast>)][
  Consider any two-player zero-sum game with utility matrix $mU in RR^(m times n)$ for Player~1. Suppose that both players use optimistic predictions, $vm_1^((t)) = mU vy^((t-1))$ and $vm_2^((t)) = -mU^top vx^((t-1))$ for $t >= 2$, with arbitrary first-round predictions $vm_1^((1)), vm_2^((1))$ (for example, zero), and regret minimizers that satisfy the RVU bound of @sec-rvu with constants $Omega_1, Omega_2$ and a common learning rate $eta$. If $eta <= 1\/(4 norm(mU)_"op")$, then at any time $T$,
  $
    "Reg"_1^((T)) + "Reg"_2^((T)) <= (Omega_1 + Omega_2) / eta + eta (norm(vg_1^((1)) - vm_1^((1)))_(cX,*)^2 + norm(vg_2^((1)) - vm_2^((1)))_(cY,*)^2),
  $
  which is _constant_ with respect to time. By the #lecture-link("learning_intro", <thm-regret-gap>)[regret-to-saddle-point-gap identity], the saddle-point gap of the average strategies $(overline(vx)^((T)), overline(vy)^((T)))$ is therefore $O_T (1\/T)$.
]
#proof[
  For $t >= 2$, the prediction error of Player~1 is $vg_1^((t)) - vm_1^((t)) = mU (vy^((t)) - vy^((t-1)))$, so by definition of the operator norm $norm(vg_1^((t)) - vm_1^((t)))_(cX,*)^2 <= norm(mU)_"op"^2 norm(vy^((t)) - vy^((t-1)))_cY^2$, and symmetrically for Player~2. Separating the first round, the RVU bounds give
  $
    "Reg"_1^((T)) &<= Omega_1 / eta + eta norm(vg_1^((1)) - vm_1^((1)))_(cX,*)^2 + eta norm(mU)_"op"^2 sum_(t=2)^T norm(vy^((t)) - vy^((t-1)))_cY^2 - 1 / (8 eta) sum_(t=2)^T norm(vx^((t)) - vx^((t-1)))_cX^2, \
    "Reg"_2^((T)) &<= Omega_2 / eta + eta norm(vg_2^((1)) - vm_2^((1)))_(cY,*)^2 + eta norm(mU)_"op"^2 sum_(t=2)^T norm(vx^((t)) - vx^((t-1)))_cX^2 - 1 / (8 eta) sum_(t=2)^T norm(vy^((t)) - vy^((t-1)))_cY^2.
  $
  Summing, each movement sum appears with coefficient $eta norm(mU)_"op"^2 - 1\/(8 eta)$, which is nonpositive whenever $eta <= 1\/(sqrt(8) norm(mU)_"op")$. The assumption $eta <= 1\/(4 norm(mU)_"op")$ is a (conservative) sufficient condition, and dropping the nonpositive terms gives the statement.
]

== Accelerated learning of coarse correlated equilibria in general games <sec-fast-cce>

In a game with $n$ players, if every player's regret after $T$ rounds is at most $R(T)$, then the empirical distribution of play is an $R(T)\/T$-approximate #lecture-link("correlated", <sec-cce>)[coarse correlated equilibrium (CCE)]. Fast rates for CCE are therefore fast rates for the _individual_ regret of each player. Note that this is a statement about the _average_ of the joint play, not about the strategies played at time $T$, and not about Nash equilibria. This is significantly harder than the two-player zero-sum case of @sec-fast-zero-sum. There, the convergence was driven by the _sum_ of the regrets, and summing the RVU bounds made the variation terms cancel. Here, the _maximum_ of the regrets matters, and the regret of a single player can in principle be large and positive while the sum is small, because regrets can be negative.

The table below summarizes the progress on the per-player regret when all players use the same learning dynamics in an $n$-player normal-form game, with full-information (expected utility) feedback and payoffs in $[0, 1]$. We write $|A|$ for the largest number of actions of any player, and suppress universal constants. The CCE gap after $T$ rounds is the stated bound divided by $T$. In terms of the dependence on $T$ alone, the progression for general $n$-player games is
$
  sqrt(T) quad -> quad T^(1\/4) quad -> quad "polylog"(T) quad -> quad log T quad -> quad 1.
$

