A few remarks help in reading the table.

- _How the improvements were obtained._ The first three rows analyze standard optimistic algorithms, notably optimistic MWU. The later rows modify the dynamics: a lifted strategy space and a logarithmic regularizer #citep(<farina2022near>), adaptive pacing of the learning rate #citep(<soleymani2025cautious>), or _higher-order_ optimism, whose predictions are built from higher-order differences of the past feedback #citep(<liu2026constant>, <abbadi2026constant>). Soleymani and Piliouras~#citep(<soleymani2026multiplicative>) obtain constant regret with one-step optimism, applied multiplicatively to regret matching. These modifications are sufficient for the stated bounds, but they are not known to be necessary; see the recent developments below.
- _The obstacle._ Recall that the RVU bound controls the _sum_ of regrets. If each individual regret is known to be nonnegative, a bound on the sum is also a bound on each regret. Making regret (or a suitable surrogate of it) nonnegative is the purpose of the lifted construction of Farina et al.~#citep(<farina2022near>), and lifted strategy spaces are also used by the two higher-order optimism results.
- _Theory versus practice._ A smaller bound does not make an algorithm the better choice in practice. Optimistic MWU costs $O(|A|)$ per update and has no tuning beyond $eta$. Some of the newer dynamics do more work per update: the lifted log-regularized dynamics of Farina et al.~#citep(<farina2022near>) and cautious optimism compute each iterate by approximately solving a small convex problem, at a cost of $O(|A| log log T)$ per update, while the per-update cost of the higher-order methods has not been analyzed (see the comparison table of Soleymani and Piliouras~#citep(<soleymani2026multiplicative>)). Several bounds also hide large constants: for instance, the explicit bound of Liu et al.~#citep(<liu2026constant>) carries a factor $2^112$.
- _Other routes._ Clairvoyant MWU #citep(<piliouras2022beyond>) obtains $O(n log|A|)$ regret, but only along a subsequence of the iterations, so its empirical play converges to a CCE at a rate of $O(n log|A| log T\/T)$.
- _Correlated equilibria._ The same question for #lecture-link("correlated", <sec-ce>)[correlated equilibria] concerns the _swap_ regret. Chen and Peng~#citep(<chen2020hedging>) obtained a $T^(-3\/4)$ rate of convergence to CE. Anagnostides et al.~#citep(<anagnostides2022nearoptimal>) improved it to $tilde(O)(1\/T)$, with $O("polylog" T)$ swap regret, and Anagnostides et al.~#citep(<anagnostides2022uncoupled>) obtained $O(log T)$ swap regret.

#paragraph-marker() *Recent developments (September 2026).*~~
Three further preprints, not yet peer reviewed, change how the table should be read. First, Ha~#citep(<ha2026horizon>) proves that _plain_ optimistic MWU with a constant learning rate already attains individual regret bounded independently of $T$, under expected-utility feedback. The dependence on the number of players and actions is implicit and nonconstructive: the proof rests on a Noetherianity argument and gives no explicit bound. So modified regularizers, lifting, and higher-order predictions are not necessary for horizon-independent regret, although they currently give the only explicit bounds. A companion paper #citep(<ha2026logarithmic>) gives an explicit $O(sqrt(n) log|A| log T)$ bound for plain optimistic MWU. Second, Mai~#citep(<mai2026constant>) obtains _constant swap regret_, $O(sqrt(n) |A| log|A| log^(5\/2)(n|A|))$, by combining the Blum--Mansour reduction with higher-order optimism. This gives $O(1\/T)$ convergence of the empirical play to correlated equilibria.

= Convergence in iterates <sec-iterate-convergence>

The convergence results we have seen so far pertain to the _average_ strategies (either individual, or the average of the product) produced by learning dynamics. In many settings, however, the object of interest is the strategy profile that is actually played at time $T$, the _last iterate_. When learning dynamics are used as a model of agents' behavior, the average is a statistical summary that nobody plays. When self-play is used to train parametric models, such as the generators and discriminators of GANs #citep(<daskalakis2018training>), averaging the parameters of the models is not the same as averaging the strategies they induce.

Fast convergence of the average, even constant regret, says nothing by itself about the current strategies. Regret controls the time average of play, and an average can converge while the strategies themselves keep cycling. The non-optimistic dynamics of @sec-optimism are an example: their averages approach the equilibrium (up to an error of order $eta$, for a constant learning rate), while their iterates spiral away from it.

We focus on two-player zero-sum games with utility matrix $mU$ for Player~1 and strategy sets $cX, cY$, and measure progress through the #lecture-link("learning_intro", <thm-regret-gap>)[saddle-point gap]
$
  gamma(vx, vy) := max_(xhat in cX) xhat^top mU vy - min_(yhat in cY) vx^top mU yhat >= 0,
$
which is zero precisely at the saddle points, that is, at the Nash equilibria. There are three natural notions of convergence:
- _average-iterate convergence_: $gamma(overline(vx)^((T)), overline(vy)^((T))) -> 0$, where $overline(vx)^((T)) := 1/T sum_(t=1)^T vx^((t))$ and similarly for $overline(vy)^((T))$. This follows from no-regret, as seen in #lecture-link("learning_intro", <thm-regret-gap>)[];
- _best-iterate convergence_: $min_(t <= T) gamma(vx^((t)), vy^((t))) -> 0$;
- _last-iterate convergence_: $gamma(vx^((T)), vy^((T))) -> 0$.
Last-iterate convergence implies best-iterate convergence. For each notion, one can ask for convergence in the limit (_asymptotic_ convergence), for a rate that may depend on the particular game (a _game-dependent_ rate), or for a rate that depends only on the number of actions and the range of the payoffs (a _uniform_ rate).

#paragraph-marker() *Why two-player zero-sum games?*~~
Suppose some learning dynamics, with polynomial-time updates, guaranteed that the last iterate is an $epsilon$-approximate Nash equilibrium after a number of iterations polynomial in the size of the game and $1\/epsilon$, in _every_ general-sum game. Running the dynamics and outputting the last iterate would then find approximate Nash equilibria in polynomial time. Under standard complexity assumptions, this is ruled out by the PPAD-hardness of computing approximate Nash equilibria, discussed in #lecture-link("ppad_completeness", none)[] #citep(<dgp09>, <chen2009settling>). The argument excludes only such _uniform, efficient_ guarantees over all games. It does not exclude last-iterate convergence in particular games or classes of games, nor guarantees that are asymptotic or whose rates depend on the game. It also does not apply to two-player zero-sum games, whose equilibria are computable in polynomial time by #lecture-link("correlated", <sec-zero-sum>)[linear programming]. Average-iterate convergence to CCE, by contrast, holds in every game.

#remark[Stronger obstructions][
  The hardness persists for _constant_ approximation: there is a constant $epsilon > 0$ for which computing an $epsilon$-approximate Nash equilibrium is PPAD-complete, already in polymatrix games with two actions per player #citep(<rubinstein2018inapproximability>). In two-player $n times n$ games, under the exponential-time hypothesis for PPAD, it requires time $n^(log^(1 - o(1)) n)$ #citep(<rubinstein2016settling>). There are also impossibility results that do not rely on complexity assumptions, but they concern specific classes of dynamical systems rather than arbitrary algorithms. No continuous-time, deterministic, uncoupled dynamics converge to Nash equilibrium in every game #citep(<hart2003uncoupled>). For dynamics that map the current mixed-strategy profile to the next one (in discrete or continuous time), there are games in which every such dynamics has starting points that do not converge to the set of Nash equilibria #citep(<Milionis2023Oct>). Beyond two-player zero-sum games, last-iterate guarantees are known for special classes, such as constant-sum polymatrix, strategically zero-sum, and potential games #citep(<Anagnostides22:Last-Iterate>), and smooth monotone games #citep(<Cai22:Finite>).
]

#paragraph-marker() *Average versus last iterate.*~~
The figure below shows the gap of both the average and the last iterates for the dynamics of the example in @sec-optimism. The non-optimistic dynamics spiral away from the interior equilibrium (@sec-negative-momentum), so their last iterates do not converge. Their averages fare better, but with a constant learning rate the regret bounds only guarantee a gap of order $eta$: the average of OGD stalls around $3 dot.c 10^(-2)$, while that of MWU is still slowly decreasing at $T = 10^4$. With MWU, the last iterate approaches the boundary of the strategy space, consistently with the analysis of Bailey and Piliouras~#citep(<bailey2018multiplicative>). The optimistic dynamics converge in the last iterate, at a linear rate, consistently with the asymptotic factor computed in @sec-negative-momentum for OGD. The optimistic averages converge at the $O(1\/T)$ rate of @sec-fast-zero-sum, which is much slower than the last iterate in this game.

