#paragraph-marker() *Best-iterate convergence.*~~
Anagnostides et al.~#citep(<Anagnostides22:Last-Iterate>) showed that when both players use optimistic gradient ascent (more generally, optimistic mirror descent with a smooth regularizer), the _best_ iterate converges to the Nash equilibrium in two-player zero-sum games at the rate of $O_T (1\/sqrt(T))$. The proof has two steps. First, writing $vz^((t)) := (vx^((t)), vy^((t)))$, the negative terms in the RVU bounds, exactly as in the proof of @sec-fast-zero-sum, show that the total movement is bounded:
$
  sum_(t=2)^T norm(vz^((t)) - vz^((t-1)))^2 <= C quad "and hence" quad min_(2 <= t <= T) norm(vz^((t)) - vz^((t-1))) <= sqrt(C / (T - 1)),
$
for a constant $C$ independent of $T$. Second, under suitable conditions, a small movement implies a small equilibrium gap: if neither player moves much in response to the current feedback, then neither can gain much by deviating. Combining the two steps, some iterate has gap $O(1\/sqrt(T))$. Both steps require only elementary calculations; feel free to try to reproduce the result yourself or check the details in the original paper. The authors note that their argument does not appear to extend to the negative entropy regularizer, that is, to optimistic MWU.

#paragraph-marker() *Last-iterate convergence.*~~
The best-iterate argument does not give last-iterate convergence: knowing that _some_ movement is small says nothing about the _last_ one. One needs a measure of progress that never increases along the dynamics. Cai et al.~#citep(<Cai22:Finite>) found such a potential and showed that the _last_ iterate of optimistic OGD converges at the rate of $O_T (1\/sqrt(T))$, for arbitrary convex constraint sets and, more generally, in smooth monotone games; the same holds for the extragradient method. Their potential is (a variant of) the _tangent residual_: the norm of the gradient after removing the components blocked by the constraints active at the current point. It is not the saddle-point gap itself, but the gap is at most the tangent residual times the diameter of the strategy sets, so a bound on the residual transfers to the gap. The proof that the potential never increases was found with the help of sum-of-squares programming.

#remark[Further results on optimistic OGD][
  The $O(1\/sqrt(T))$ rate is tight: Golowich et al.~#citep(<golowich2020last>) and Golowich et al.~#citep(<golowich2020tight>) proved a matching lower bound for the last iterate of a class of methods that includes optimistic OGD and extragradient, in contrast with the $O(1\/T)$ rate of the average iterate. In the unconstrained setting, the $O(1\/sqrt(T))$ rate for optimistic OGD was obtained earlier by Golowich et al.~#citep(<golowich2020tight>), under an additional smoothness assumption on the Jacobian of the game's gradient operator, which Gorbunov et al.~#citep(<gorbunov2022last>) removed with a computer-aided analysis.
]

Neither uniform result above covers optimistic MWU: the best-iterate argument requires a smooth regularizer, and the last-iterate analysis concerns optimistic OGD and extragradient.

Arguably, it was believed for a while in the community that good last-iterate convergence rates for OMWU were in the air, just "one good trick" away. After all, OMWU had always spoiled us with its good properties. Last-iterate convergence of OMWU was known asymptotically when the equilibrium is unique #citep(<daskalakis2019last>), and even at a linear rate in that case #citep(<wei2021linear>). However, that rate depends on a game-dependent constant, and it seemed pretty likely that a uniform rate could be established, as for optimistic OGD. In a recent twist, it was shown that the situation is less rosy than expected. We illustrate this with an example.

#example[Poor last-iterate convergence of OMWU, #citep(<Cai2024Jun>)][
  Consider the two-player zero-sum game in which Player~1 maximizes $vx^top mU(delta) vy$ and Player~2 maximizes $-vx^top mU(delta) vy$, where
  $ mU(delta) := mat(1/2 - delta, 1/2; 1, 0), qquad 0 < delta < 1/2. $
  Cai et al.~#citep(<Cai2024Jun>) describe the same game through the _loss_ matrix $mL(delta) := mat(1/2 + delta, 1/2; 0, 1)$, which Player~1 minimizes and Player~2 maximizes. The two descriptions are related by $mU(delta) = mJ - mL(delta)$, where $mJ$ is the all-ones matrix. Since $vx^top mJ vy = 1$ for all mixed strategies, $vx^top mU(delta) vy = 1 - vx^top mL(delta) vy$, so both descriptions define the same game. They also induce identical learning dynamics: Player~1's utility vector $mU(delta) vy = bold(1) - mL(delta) vy$ is the negated loss vector plus a constant vector, and adding a constant to every coordinate changes neither the entropic nor the Euclidean updates on the simplex (and similarly for Player~2). The game admits the unique Nash equilibrium $(vx^*, vy^*)$, where $ vx^* = (1/(1+delta), delta/(1+delta)) qquad "and" qquad vy^* = (1/(2(1+delta)), (1+2delta)/(2(1+delta))). $
  In particular, when $delta$ is small, the equilibrium strategy for Player 1 is approximately $vx^* = (1 - delta, delta)$ and thus very close to the boundary of the strategy polytope of the player. This proximity to the boundary affects the performance of all known instantiations of the optimistic FTRL algorithm. To see this numerically, we run three optimistic FTRL variants, with the negative entropy (that is, optimistic MWU), the squared Euclidean norm, and the logarithmic regularizer $-sum_a log x(a)$, together with optimistic OGD, in the game with $delta = 10^(-2)$. All four use the learning rate $eta = 0.1$ and start from uniform strategies.

  #align(center, image(
