#paragraph-marker() *The mechanism: a lack of forgetfulness.*~~
For two actions, optimistic MWU is easiest to read through the log-odds of the strategy. Writing $Delta g^((t)) := g^((t))(1) - g^((t))(2)$ for a player's utility difference between its two actions (with $Delta g^((0)) := 0$), the update is
$
  log x_1^((t+1)) / x_2^((t+1)) = eta (sum_(tau=1)^t Delta g^((tau)) + Delta g^((t))),
$
or, equivalently,
$
  log x_1^((t+1)) / x_2^((t+1)) = log x_1^((t)) / x_2^((t)) + eta (2 Delta g^((t)) - Delta g^((t-1))).
$
The log-odds track the _cumulative_ utility difference. A long run of feedback favoring one action makes the strategy exponentially concentrated on it, and undoing this requires the cumulative sum to come back, which takes many rounds if each round can move it only a little. In the game above, Player~2's utility difference is $Delta g_2^((t)) = delta x_1^((t)) - x_2^((t)) <= delta$, so whatever Player~1 does, Player~2's cumulative difference moves towards its first action by at most $delta$ per round. Following Cai et al.~#citep(<Cai2024Jun>), the dynamics go through three phases, separated by two times (marked $times$ and #sym.square.filled in the figures).
- _Approach_, until $T_1$, the first time at which $x_1^((t)) >= 1\/(1 + delta)$: Player~1 moves towards its first action and Player~2 towards its second, and the gap drops.
- _Plateau_, from $T_1$ to $T_2$, the first later time at which $y_1^((t)) >= 1\/(2(1 + delta))$: Player~2 needs $Omega(1\/delta)$ rounds to "forget" its history, while Player~1 is pushed ever closer to the boundary.
- _Rebound_, after $T_2$: Player~2 returns to its first action while Player~1 is still extremely close to the boundary, and the gap jumps back to a constant.
The figure below repeats the experiment with optimistic MWU for three values of $delta$. Cai et al.~#citep(<Cai2024Jun>) prove that the plateau lasts $Omega(1\/delta)$ rounds. In the simulation, the product $T_2 delta$ stays between 26 and 28, which is consistent with a plateau length proportional to $1\/delta$; a simulation illustrates this scaling but does not prove it. Optimistic OGD, which _does_ forget, reaches a gap of $10^(-6)$ after about 5,000 to 6,000 iterations for all three values of $delta$.

