By studying the dynamics produced by instantiations of the FTRL algorithm in the previous game, the following result can be established.

#theorem[Informal, #citep(<Cai2024Jun>)][
  Under standard assumptions about the regularizer, which hold for the negative entropy, the squared Euclidean norm, the logarithmic regularizer, and the Tsallis entropies, there is no function $f$ such that optimistic FTRL with a constant learning rate produces a last-iterate convergence rate of $f(|A_1|, |A_2|, T) -> 0$ when all payoffs of the game are in $[0, 1]$, where $|A_1|$ and $|A_2|$ are the numbers of actions of the players. More precisely, for every sufficiently small $delta > 0$, the equilibrium gap of the iterate is a positive constant at some time $t >= Omega(1\/delta)$ in the game above. In other words, the last-iterate convergence rate must depend on _some form of condition number_ of the game.
]

#remark[What the theorem does not say][
  The theorem does _not_ say that optimistic MWU fails to converge. In every fixed game, with a small enough constant learning rate, its last iterate converges asymptotically #citep(<daskalakis2019last>, <orabona2026last>), and linearly when the equilibrium is unique #citep(<wei2021linear>). What fails is a _uniform_ rate: as $delta -> 0$, the time needed grows without bound. The answer also depends on how convergence is measured. Lazarsfeld et al.~#citep(<lazarsfeld2026when>) give a sharp game-dependent linear rate in KL divergence for games with a unique interior equilibrium, and, for $2 times 2$ games, a uniform $tilde(O)(T^(-1\/2))$ rate for the _best_ iterate in duality gap. They also show that no uniform best-iterate rate exists in KL divergence or total variation distance.
]

Since optimistic OGD _does_ have a uniform rate, the theorem separates the two families of optimistic algorithms. Optimistic MWU retains its advantages for _average_ iterates, namely a logarithmic dependence on the number of actions, and fast rates to CCE in general games (@sec-fast-cce). For uniform last-iterate rates, forgetting the past appears to be necessary. Cai et al.~#citep(<Cai2024Jun>) conjecture that the slow last-iterate convergence of optimistic FTRL persists with decreasing or adaptive learning rates.

